RESOLVED (Slice 69): fixed in %loop-expand.  WHILE/UNTIL now emit
`(when <test> (go end-tag))` inline in `body` at their textual position (the
end-tag is gensym'd up front so they can target it), and top-level accumulation
updates (COLLECT/APPEND/NCONC/SUM/COUNT/MAX/MIN) are emitted into `body` at
position too (via a new add-accum helper + %loop-accum-body-kind) so they
interleave correctly with WHILE/UNTIL/DO.  COLLECT vars are now reversed once at
loop end (so FINALLY / INTO / result are all forward-order).  Verified on a clean
build under normal GC: the prelude loads and a conformance battery passes
(`do X while Y`, `collect X while Y`, `collect X until Y`, `collect into +
finally`, sum/count/append/when-collect, etc.).
Known remaining edge case (separate, pre-existing): `if X collect A into a else
collect B into b` leaves the else INTO var unbound (the ELSE handler reuses the
WHEN-branch var instead of registering a distinct one).

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOOP evaluates a trailing :WHILE / :UNTIL clause BEFORE the body of the same
iteration, instead of at its textual position.  Per CLHS 6.1.1.4 / 6.1.9, the
main loop clauses execute in the order written, so a WHILE/UNTIL placed AFTER a
DO/COLLECT is a *post-test* (the body clause runs, then the test); bocl makes
every WHILE/UNTIL a *pre-test* at the top of the loop body.

REPRODUCE (bocl gives the second column; a conforming LOOP gives the first):
  (loop for i from 1 to 3 do (princ i) while (< i 2))   "12"     -> bocl "1"
  (loop for i from 1 to 3 collect i while (< i 2))       (1 2)    -> bocl (1)
  (loop for i from 1 to 3 do (princ i) until (>= i 3))   "123"    -> bocl "12"

ROOT CAUSE (bootstrap/prelude.lisp, %loop-expand):
  WHILE/UNTIL push their test onto `tests` (lines ~2183-2187), and code
  generation emits ALL of `tests` as `test-forms` at the TOP of the loop body,
  before `body`:
        top-tag
          ,@test-forms      ; <-- every while/until, hoisted here
          ,@setup-forms
          ,@body            ; do / collect / ...
          ,@accum-forms
          ,@step-forms
          (go top-tag)
        end-tag ...
  So a WHILE/UNTIL written after a DO/COLLECT is tested before it.  (The FOR /
  REPEAT termination tests legitimately belong at the top; only WHILE/UNTIL must
  move.)

FIX (sketch):
  Create end-tag as a gensym BEFORE the parse loop, and when parsing
  WHILE/UNTIL append `(when <test> (go ,end-tag))` into `body` at the current
  position instead of pushing to `tests`.  Leave FOR/REPEAT tests in `tests`
  (top of loop).  Reuse the pre-created end-tag in codegen.
  Test load-bearing: LOOP is used throughout the prelude, so re-verify the whole
  prelude loads and the ANSI LOOP tests after the change.

IMPACT
  - ANSI conformance: any code relying on post-test WHILE/UNTIL is wrong.
  - Surfaced while integrating the Lisp bignum engine (bootstrap/bignum.lisp):
    u%plus and u%times-digit used `:collect/:do ... :while ...` to flush the
    final carry, which bocl dropped, corrupting bignum * and +.  As an interim
    measure those two functions were rewritten in pre-test form (Slice that
    cleaned bignum.lisp), but the LOOP bug itself remains and should be fixed.
